---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Programming Basics</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 1.1 &ndash; 1.9. Use this to review, or to fill in a
          definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> How a Computer Works</h2>

          <p>
            Every computer does four things: takes <b>input</b>, does <b
              >processing</b
            >, produces <b>output</b>, and uses <b>memory</b> to hold data along
            the way. Underneath all of it, data is stored in <b>binary</b>.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Input</dt>
            <dd>Information a computer receives from the outside world.</dd>
            <dt>Output</dt>
            <dd>Information a computer sends back out to the world.</dd>
            <dt>Processing (CPU)</dt>
            <dd>The part of the computer that carries out instructions.</dd>
            <dt>Memory</dt>
            <dd>
              Where a computer holds data, either temporarily (RAM) or long-term
              (storage).
            </dd>
            <dt>Binary</dt>
            <dd>
              A system of representing information using only two states, on and
              off.
            </dd>
            <dt>Bit</dt>
            <dd>A single binary digit, either 0 or 1.</dd>
            <dt>Byte</dt>
            <dd>A group of eight bits.</dd>
            <dt>Programming Language</dt>
            <dd>
              A structured set of rules for writing instructions a computer can
              run.
            </dd>
            <dt>Code Editor</dt>
            <dd>A program built for writing, testing, and running code.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> Output and Comments</h2>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="print()"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            /> displays text in the console. Anything after a{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="#"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            /> is a comment and is ignored when the program runs.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Function</dt>
            <dd>Code that performs a specific action.</dd>
            <dt>Console Output</dt>
            <dd>
              Text displayed by a program in a command-line interface, like a
              terminal.
            </dd>
            <dt>Comment</dt>
            <dd>
              A note written within the code that is ignored by Python, used to
              explain or clarify the code.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> Variables and Data Types</h2>

          <p>
            A variable is a name for a stored value. Every value has a type, and
            you can check it with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="type()"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Variable</dt>
            <dd>A named location that stores a value for later use.</dd>
            <dt>Assignment</dt>
            <dd>
              Storing a value in a variable using the{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="="
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> operator.
            </dd>
            <dt>Data Type</dt>
            <dd>
              The kind of value a variable holds, such as text or a number.
            </dd>
            <dt>String (str)</dt>
            <dd>A data type representing text.</dd>
            <dt>Integer (int)</dt>
            <dd>A data type representing a whole number.</dd>
            <dt>Float (float)</dt>
            <dd>A data type representing a decimal number.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> Arithmetic</h2>

          <p>
            Python evaluates expressions using order of operations, and
            parentheses override it. Beyond the four basic operators there are{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="//"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />,{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="%"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="**"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Operator</dt>
            <dd>
              A symbol that performs an operation, such as{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="+"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> or{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="*"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </dd>
            <dt>Operand</dt>
            <dd>A value an operator works on.</dd>
            <dt>Expression</dt>
            <dd>
              A combination of values and operators that produces a result.
            </dd>
            <dt>Floor Division</dt>
            <dd>
              Division that drops any decimal remainder, using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="//"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </dd>
            <dt>Modulus</dt>
            <dd>
              The remainder left over after division, using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="%"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </dd>
            <dt>Order of Operations</dt>
            <dd>
              The rules that decide which part of an expression is evaluated
              first.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">5.</span> Changing Values Over Time</h2>

          <p>
            A variable can be given a new value at any point, including one
            calculated from its own current value. What your variables hold at a
            given moment is the program's state.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Reassignment</dt>
            <dd>Giving an existing variable a new value.</dd>
            <dt>Self-Referential Update</dt>
            <dd>
              An assignment where a variable's current value is used to compute
              its new value, like{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="health = health - 20"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </dd>
            <dt>State</dt>
            <dd>
              The current value(s) a program is keeping track of at a given
              moment.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">6.</span> f-Strings</h2>

          <p>
            An f-string puts a variable or an expression directly inside a
            string, instead of joining pieces together by hand.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>f-String</dt>
            <dd>
              A string prefixed with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="f"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> that can insert variables or expressions directly inside it.
            </dd>
            <dt>Placeholder</dt>
            <dd>
              The{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="{ }"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> spot inside an f-string where a value gets inserted.
            </dd>
            <dt>String Interpolation</dt>
            <dd>Inserting a variable's value directly into a string.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">7.</span> Input and Type Casting</h2>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="input()"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            /> always returns a string, even when the user types digits. To do math
            with it, cast it first with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="int()"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            /> or{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="float()"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Console Input</dt>
            <dd>
              Text a user types into the terminal while a program is running.
            </dd>
            <dt>input()</dt>
            <dd>
              A function that displays a message and returns whatever the user
              types, as a string.
            </dd>
            <dt>Prompt</dt>
            <dd>The message shown to the user asking for input.</dd>
            <dt>Type Casting</dt>
            <dd>Converting a value from one data type to another.</dd>
            <dt>int()</dt>
            <dd>Converts a value into a whole number.</dd>
            <dt>float()</dt>
            <dd>Converts a value into a decimal number.</dd>
            <dt>Nested Function Call</dt>
            <dd>
              A function call placed inside another function call, evaluated
              from the inside out.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">8.</span> Modules and Math</h2>

          <p>
            Tools that aren't built into Python come from modules you bring in
            with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="import"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. The{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="math"
              lang={"python"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            /> module was the first one you used.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Module</dt>
            <dd>
              A collection of tools not built directly into Python &mdash; you
              bring it in with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="import"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </dd>
            <dt>Constant</dt>
            <dd>
              A named value, like{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="math.pi"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, that doesn't change &mdash; read directly, not called with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="()"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </dd>
            <dt>round()</dt>
            <dd>
              Built-in function that rounds a number, optionally to a specific
              number of decimal places.
            </dd>
            <dt>math.pow()</dt>
            <dd>
              Raises a number to a power, same job as{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="**"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, but always returns a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="float"
                lang={"python"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </dd>
            <dt>math.sqrt()</dt>
            <dd>Returns the square root of a non-negative number.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
